window.SEARCH_INDEX=[{"path":"chapters/template.html","title":"Chapter template — The Agentic Coding Field Manual","text":"part I · tools & access · L1 coral Chapter N: Title goes here One-line promise: what the reader can DO after this chapter. facts checked as of YYYY-MM-DD Generalized first, always The technique is taught tool-agnostically before any specific harness is named. This section would explain the concept, why it matters, and the mental model — readable by someone who has never installed anything. tip Practitioner shortcuts live here. Earned, not invented. warning Common failure modes and how to recognize them early. grey zone · risk-flagged Anything ToS-adjacent or unofficial lives in risk callouts: what it is, why people do it, what breaks, what it could cost you. Never recommended without the warning attached. Demonstration on the build The same technique applied inside the current threaded project, with real commands, real output, and honest notes about what went wrong the first time. $ command that a reader can paste verbatim expected output shown honestly, including errors Receipts Every factual claim above traces to a source card like these: T1 official https://opencode.ai/docs/skills checked 2026-08-24 T2 vendor blog https://example.com/marketing-post checked 2026-08-24 · marketing claims flagged inline T3 lead only https://example.com/forum-rumor never cited as fact — pointer for your own verification Try it yourself Exact-click checkpoint. Open X, click Y — written so a non-coder follows mechanically. Break it on purpose. Change one thing, predict the result, check your prediction. Ship-sized task. Apply today's technique to your own project, not just the book's. Self-check Comprehension question one? Answer, in plain language. Comprehension question two? Answer. When would this technique be the wrong choice? Answer. Beyond this build Where else this pattern applies, which other harnesses support it (and how their mechanics differ), and the edge cases where it breaks down. This block is the generalization guarantee — never omit it. ← contents next chapter →"},{"path":"index.html","title":"The Agentic Coding Field Manual — अ Man Learns Things","text":"अ Man Learns Things · a field manual · scaffold v1 The Agentic Coding Field Manual Master the modern agentic workflow — prompting patterns that survive production, UI craft, testing discipline, daily habits — with coding agents like opencode, Claude Code, Codex and Gemini CLI. $0-first tools, receipts behind every claim. Written for people who learn by building, not by reading theory. Open the contents Watch it being built 16 chapters 3 real builds $0-first tools blind-critic quality gates The method that wrote this book Every chapter is ground through the same loop you'll learn inside it: a builder agent makes a piece, a separate critic with fresh context compares ours against the real reference blind, picks a winner, names the biggest gap, and sends it back. The loop only stops when ours wins. builder writes the piece → critic fresh context, harsh, binary blind A/B vs bar → biggest gap named, sent back loop until ours wins — never a round count Three real builds carry the book You won't practice on toys. Three projects from Aman's own build specs get constructed chapter by chapter as the book teaches — each one $0-native, each one shipped. build 1 · weekend Agent Cost Compass Paste your agent's token pattern, see what a run costs across ~20 LLM providers. Your first agent build — configured, prompted, verified, shipped. build 2 · two-week NCERT Quiz Forge Quizzes + spaced repetition for NCERT textbooks. Where UI craft, testing discipline and human-review gates get learned for real. build 3 · six-week capstone Free-Tier Atlas The IMDb of free tiers — change-tracking, watchlists, community submissions. Pipelines, anti-bloat ops and honest grey zones, at capstone scale. Generalized, not project-bound Every chapter teaches the technique tool-agnostically first, demonstrates it on the build, then closes with Beyond this build : where else the pattern applies, which other tools it works with, and where it breaks. The coverage matrix in docs/ guarantees nothing gets dropped because a project didn't happen to need it."},{"path":"progress.html","title":"Progress — The Agentic Coding Field Manual","text":"Progress The book is built in phases, each phase a fresh gauntlet run that resumes from disk. This board is the human view of STATUS.md — agents update both together at every milestone. last updated: 2026-08-25 · P1 research wave complete Done P0 · shipped Scaffold Repo + local git, design tokens (light + dark), site skeleton, client-side search, theme toggle, coverage matrix, governance files. P1 · shipped Research wave Six research agents vetted sources per chapter cluster. ~160 tiered rows in the source ledger; every future citation comes from it. Ten gaps logged with binding drafting instructions. In transit gate · now Ledger review Aman reviews docs/source-ledger.md, then P2 begins: chapters 1–4 drafted against the ledger while Build 1 (agent-cost-compass) runs its own BUILD.md kickoff. Not yet built P2 Foundations + Build 1 Chapters 1–4 gauntleted against the bars; agent-cost-compass woven in. P3 Craft + Build 2 Chapters 5–11; ncert-quiz-forge built. P4 Ship & sanity + Build 3 Chapters 12–16; free-tier-atlas capstone. P5 Integration & launch Cross-links, full-book blind judging vs both bars, push to GitHub, Pages live. How quality is enforced builder agent fresh context → critic agent fetches real bars blind A/B, labels stripped → verdict winner + biggest gap bars: ciechanow.ski (visual) · code.claude.com best practices (content)"},{"path":"toc.html","title":"Contents — The Agentic Coding Field Manual","text":"Contents Six parts on six route lines. Statuses update as phases land; rows become links when their chapter ships. part 0 · orientation · L0 saffron 01 What agentic coding actually is planned Harness vs model vs loop. Mental models that survive contact with real work, the failure modes nobody blogs about, and when NOT to reach for an agent. part I · tools & access · $0 core · L1 coral 02 The harness landscape planned opencode deep-dive; Claude Code, Codex CLI, Gemini CLI, Cline/Roo, Goose, Aider, Pi compared on what matters at $0. 03 Models without money planned Free tiers, OpenRouter free models, open-weight GLM/Qwen/Kimi/ DeepSeek, local via Ollama & LM Studio, subscription reuse, rate-limit reality. 04 Configuring your agent properly planned AGENTS.md & memory files, skills, custom commands, MCP servers, hooks as guardrails, permissions, LSP diagnostics, plan mode, sessions and worktrees. part II · prompting craft · L2 olive 05 Context engineering planned What enters context, compaction, memory across sessions, token discipline, context rot and how to fight it. 06 Patterns that ship planned Plan-first, spec-driven development, gauntlet loops and evaluator patterns, TDD with agents, subagent fan-out, verification loops, anti-patterns. 07 Source literacy for agent builders planned Reliable vs unreliable sources, how benchmarks get gamed, verifying claims yourself in minutes. part III · building real things · L3 jade 08 Good UI with agents planned Design tokens and systems, screenshot-compare loops, component contracts, accessibility as a default not an afterthought. 09 Testing what agents build planned Test strategy for agent output, browser automation, review gates a non-coder can run, evals-lite. 10 Debugging when it breaks planned Systematic debugging with agents, bisection, reading logs, escape hatches when the loop spins. 11 Brownfield reality planned Working inside large existing codebases: understand-before-edit, safe refactors, mapping unfamiliar territory. part IV · shipping & staying sane · L4 plum 12 Pipelines that prevent bloat planned Branching discipline, CI gates, agent code review, dependency hygiene, refactor loops, docs drift. 13 Shipping for $0 planned GitHub Pages, Cloudflare Pages, Vercel free paths, preview deploys, release notes written by agents. 14 Security & secrets planned Prompt injection, secret leakage into context, malicious MCP servers and skill supply chains, permission deny lists. 15 Costs, limits & grey zones planned One compact reference chapter: rate-limit math, spend caps, free-tier tradeoffs and ToS boundaries — the honest footnote, not the theme. 16 Your daily agentic workflow planned The capstone habit: session rituals, memory upkeep, parallel worktrees, review rhythm — how competent people actually work with agents every day. appendices · reference shelf · L5 rose A Tool cheat sheet planned One-page-per-harness command and config reference. B The source ledger planned Every source cited in the book, tiered and dated. C Glossary planned Harness, context window, MCP, hook, skill — plain definitions. D Coverage matrix planned Dimensions × chapters — proof of exhaustiveness, living document."}];
